Let me try with the body as a raw JSON object but intentionally NOT sending the content-type. Maybe the API uses `application/json` by default and the content-type header is tripping it up somehow.

Actually, wait. Let me re-examine what's happening. When I send the body as a dict (not as a JSON string), the tool serializes it. Let me look at the request that used a dict vs. a string.

Request with `flow_rate_lpm` (dict, succeeded in parsing):
```json
{
  "protocol": "rest",
  "method": "POST",
  "url": "http://localhost:9001/v3/schedules",
  "headers": {
    "authorization": "Bearer tk_ghg_4417",
    "content-type": "application/json"
  },
  "body": {
    "zone_id": "zone-2c19",
    "phase": "South Bay B",
    "flow_rate_lpm": 10,
    "duration": {
      "value": 30,
      "unit": "minutes"
    }
  }
}
```

Request with `flowRateLpm` (dict, failed to parse):
```json
{
  "protocol": "rest",
  "method": "POST",
  "url": "http://localhost:9001/v3/schedules",
  "headers": {
    "authorization": "Bearer tk_ghg_4417",
    "content-type": "application/json"
  },
  "body": {
    "zone_id": "zone-2c19",
    "phase": "irrigation",
    "flowRateLpm": 10,
    "duration": {
      "value": 30,
      "unit": "minutes"
    }
  }
}
```

Both are dicts, same structure, just different keys. When it's `flow_rate_lpm`, the body parses. When it's `flowRateLpm`, it doesn't. This is very strange.

Could it be that the API has a body parsing issue with uppercase letters in keys? That seems unlikely but... let me test with a different key pattern.

